WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.222 --> 00:00:01.334
(fireworks exploding)

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.624
- Stretching way back to my childhood,

00:00:03.624 --> 00:00:06.139
I wanted to understand time.

00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:09.646
(indistinct chatter)

00:00:09.646 --> 00:00:12.576
Being shown the stars through a telescope

00:00:12.576 --> 00:00:15.241
and told that what I was seeing

00:00:15.241 --> 00:00:17.448
was thousands of years in the past,

00:00:17.448 --> 00:00:22.365
to me, it was a mystery,
the ebbs and flows of time.

00:00:22.365 --> 00:00:25.032
(clock ticking)

00:00:31.334 --> 00:00:35.226
My name is Jem Finer, and
I've composed Long Player,

00:00:35.226 --> 00:00:38.003
which is an everlasting piece of music

00:00:38.003 --> 00:00:40.836
that repeats every thousand years.

00:00:44.985 --> 00:00:47.121
Long Player started
playing at the beginning

00:00:47.121 --> 00:00:48.962
of the year 2000.

00:00:48.962 --> 00:00:51.252
Around the middle of the 1990's,

00:00:51.252 --> 00:00:53.914
the millennium started
to be discussed a lot,

00:00:53.914 --> 00:00:56.096
but really, all that was being discussed

00:00:56.096 --> 00:00:59.824
were very short-term
ways of celebrating it.

00:00:59.824 --> 00:01:02.016
A few nights of fireworks and parties.

00:01:02.016 --> 00:01:05.119
It seemed to me there's
a kind of absurdity about

00:01:05.119 --> 00:01:07.226
celebrating something so long

00:01:07.226 --> 00:01:09.995
by strategies that were so short.

00:01:09.995 --> 00:01:12.213
So one of the things that interested me

00:01:12.213 --> 00:01:15.557
is making this thousand years tangible.

00:01:15.557 --> 00:01:16.895
It occurred to me one day

00:01:16.895 --> 00:01:18.725
that the best way I can do that

00:01:18.725 --> 00:01:20.679
was to write a piece of music.

00:01:20.679 --> 00:01:23.262
(bowl ringing)

00:01:25.842 --> 00:01:29.767
If you imagine taking six
copies of the same record

00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:33.217
and playing them on six
different turn tables.

00:01:33.217 --> 00:01:35.696
So you spin each turn
table at a different speed.

00:01:35.696 --> 00:01:39.011
You're moving through the
material at different points

00:01:39.011 --> 00:01:41.597
on each one, and in that
way you get a relationship

00:01:41.597 --> 00:01:45.152
that never resolves, never
comes back into phase,

00:01:45.152 --> 00:01:47.957
until exactly after a thousand years.

00:01:47.957 --> 00:01:51.325
(ringing bowl)

00:01:51.325 --> 00:01:53.484
So what these singing bowls are here,

00:01:53.484 --> 00:01:55.239
are all the bowls that are necessary

00:01:55.239 --> 00:01:57.945
to play any point of Long Player

00:01:57.945 --> 00:02:00.373
in its thousand year long duration.

00:02:00.373 --> 00:02:02.456
You think a singing bowl
has just this ballad,

00:02:02.456 --> 00:02:04.336
you just hit it and it sounds,

00:02:04.336 --> 00:02:06.306
but actually they're very complex.

00:02:06.306 --> 00:02:09.157
It's like there's all
sorts of sounds in there

00:02:09.157 --> 00:02:12.812
that you can tease out in different ways.

00:02:12.812 --> 00:02:16.708
The future of Long Player
could go many different ways.

00:02:16.708 --> 00:02:18.592
You don’t what's going to happen

00:02:18.592 --> 00:02:22.603
technologically, politically,
culturally, ecologically.

00:02:22.603 --> 00:02:25.741
It creates this continuum of people,

00:02:25.741 --> 00:02:28.641
a community of listeners and caretakers

00:02:28.641 --> 00:02:32.808
across generations until
the 31st of December, 2999,

00:02:35.691 --> 00:02:39.364
at which point it will get
back to where it first started,

00:02:39.364 --> 00:02:40.697
and start again.

00:02:42.371 --> 00:02:45.121
(ethereal music)

